to our experience of
life in terms of vision,

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.480
hearing, taste, et cetera?

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.480
- So the brain is an extra ordinary organ

00:09:17.708 --> 00:09:20.583
that weights maybe 2% of your body mass.

00:09:21.567 --> 00:09:25.387
Yet it consumes anywhere between 25

00:09:25.387 --> 00:09:29.823
to 30% of all of your energy and oxygen.

00:09:32.822 --> 00:09:35.020
And it gets transformed into a mind

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:40.765
and this mind changes the human condition.

00:09:40.765 --> 00:09:45.765
It make, it changes, it
transforms fear into courage,

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:54.020
conformity into creativity,
sadness into happiness.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.333
How the hell does that happen?

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.780
Now, the challenge that the brain faces

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.940
is that the world is made of real things.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.983
You know, this here is a glass,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.120
and this is a cord and
this is a microphone,

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:14.880
but the brain is only made of neurons

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.613
that only understand electrical signals.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:23.263
So how do you transform
that reality into nothing

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:27.450
that electrical signals that now need

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.883
to represent the world?

00:10:32.286 --> 00:10:33.119
And that process

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:38.043
is what we can operationally
define as perception,

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:43.380
in the senses, let's say olfactory,

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:48.380
other taste, vision we
can very straightforwardly

00:10:49.293 --> 00:10:52.410
separate detection from perception,

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:55.530
detection is what happens when
you take a sugar molecule,

00:10:55.530 --> 00:11:00.360
you put it in your tongue and
then a set of specific cells

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:04.950
now sense that sugar
molecule that's detection,

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:06.990
you haven't perceived anything yet.

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:09.243
That is just your cells in your tongue,

00:11:10.271 --> 00:11:11.880
interacting with this chemical.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.810
But now that cell gets
activated and sends a signal

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:17.250
to the brain and now detection

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:20.223
gets transformed into perception.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:25.080
And he's trying to
understand how that happens.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:30.080
That's been the maniacal drive,

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:34.861
of my entire career in neuroscience.

00:11:34.861 --> 00:11:38.700
How does the brain ultimately
transform detection

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:43.293
into perception so that it can
guide actions and behaviors?

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:45.510
Does that make sense?

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:46.560
- Absolutely.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.383
And is a very clear and
beautiful description,

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:52.500
a sort of high level
question related to that.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:53.880
And then I think we can get

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:57.081
into some of the intermediate steps.

00:11:57.081 --> 00:12:00.206
I think many people would
like to know whether

00:12:00.206 --> 00:12:01.680
or not my perception of the color

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:04.620
of your shirt is the
same as your perception

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:06.472
of the color of your shirt.

00:12:06.472 --> 00:12:07.675
- Excellent question.

00:12:07.675 --> 00:12:08.580
Am I okay to interrupt you

00:12:09.472 --> 00:12:11.988
as I'm guessing what you're going?

00:12:11.988 --> 00:12:14.040
- Interruption is welcome on this podcast.

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.260
The audience will always penalize me

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:18.078
for interrupting you,

00:12:18.078 --> 00:12:19.470
and will never penalize
you for interrupting me.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.083
- Good, I like the one way penalize.

00:12:24.302 --> 00:12:26.680
Now, given what I told you before

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:30.840
that the brain is trying to represent

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.425
the world based in nothing

00:12:33.425 --> 00:12:37.317
but the transformation of
these signals into electrical

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:43.800
languages that now neurons
had to encode and decode.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:48.000
It follows that your brain
is different than my brain.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:49.890
And therefore it follows that

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:53.430
the way that you are perceiving
the world must be different

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:57.183
than mine, even when receiving
the same sensory cues.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:12:59.523
And I'll tell you about an experiment

00:12:59.523 --> 00:13:02.730
is a simple experiment yet brilliant.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:06.180
That demonstrates why
we perceive the world,

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:09.513
how we perceive the world different.

00:13:10.461 --> 00:13:13.743
So in the world of vision, as you know,

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:19.350
well now we have three classes
of photo receptor neurons

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:24.350
that sense three basic
colors, red, blue, and green.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:28.450
Blue, green, and red

00:13:29.346 --> 00:13:31.330
if we go from short to long wavelength

00:13:32.273 --> 00:13:34.680
and these three are
sufficient to accommodate

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:36.333
the full visible spectra,

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:40.143
I'm going to take three light projectors,

00:13:41.432 --> 00:13:44.550
and I'm going to project
with one into a wide screen,

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:47.970
a red light, and the
other one green light,

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.010
I'm going to overlap the two beams.

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:51.963
And on the screen, there's be yellow.

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:54.840
This is the superposition

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:58.980
when you have two beams of red and green,

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:01.710
and then I'm going to
take a third projector

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.210
and I'm going to put a filter

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.943
that projects right
next to that mixed beam,

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.093
a spectral pure yellow.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.460
And I'm going to ask you to
come to the red and green

00:14:16.451 --> 00:14:20.280
projectors and play with intensity knobs

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:22.620
so that you can match that yellow

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:27.210
that you're projecting to the
spectrally pure next to it.

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:28.350
Is this making sense?

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:29.891
- Perfect sense.

00:14:29.891 --> 00:14:31.320
- And I'm going to write down the numbers

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:33.270
in those two volume

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:37.390
intensity knobs and then I'm going to ask

00:14:38.481 --> 00:14:39.840
the next person to do the same.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:42.480
And then I'm going to
ask every person around

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:46.890
this area of Battery Park
in New York to do the same,

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:49.860
and guess what, we're going to end up

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:53.190
with thousands of different
number combinations.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:54.300
- Amazing.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:55.590
- So for all of us,

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:59.763
is yellow enough that we
can use a common language,

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.820
but for every one of us,

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:07.113
that yellow is going to be
ever so slightly differently.

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:11.460
And so I think that simple
psychological experiment

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:12.690
beautifully illustrates

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:15.993
how we truly perceive
the world differently.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.060
- I love that example.

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:19.950
And yet, in that example,

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:23.163
we know the basic elements
from which color is created.

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:27.180
If we migrate into a
slightly different sense,

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:30.060
let me pick a hard one, like-

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:32.593
- Sound.
- Sound, olfaction.

00:15:32.593 --> 00:15:36.001
- Very hard then to do an experiment

00:15:36.001 --> 00:15:39.450
that will allow us to get that degree

00:15:39.450 --> 00:15:44.160
of granularity and beautiful causality,

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:49.160
where we can show that A
produces and leads to B.

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:51.270
If I give you the smell of a rose,

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:52.773
you can describe it to me.

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:58.080
If I smell the same rose,
I can describe it also,

00:15:58.080 --> 00:15:59.970
but I have no way whether

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:03.090
the two of us are experiencing the same,

00:16:03.090 --> 00:16:08.090
but it's close enough that
we can both pretty much say

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:12.653
that it has the following enough features

00:16:12.653 --> 00:16:14.790
or other determinants,

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:19.790
but no question that your
experience is different than mine.

00:16:21.806 --> 00:16:25.380
- The fact that it's good
enough for us to both survive

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:26.610
that your perception of yellow

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:28.560
and my perception of yellow at least up

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:30.960
until now is good enough
for us both to survive.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:32.100
- You got it.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.300
- Raises a thought

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:36.090
about a statement made by a colleague

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:39.480
of ours, Marcus Meister at Caltech.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:40.800
It's never been on this podcast,

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:45.510
but in a review that I read
by Marcus, at one point,

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:48.360
he said that the basic
function of perception

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:51.000
is to divide our behavioral responses

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:55.410
into the outcomes downstream

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.950
of three basic emotional responses.

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:00.060
Yum.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:01.410
I like it.

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:02.430
Yuck.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:03.450
I hate it.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:06.480
Or meh, whatever.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:07.410
What do you think about,

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.330
I'm not looking to establish a debate

00:17:09.330 --> 00:17:11.070
between you and Marcus,
without Marcus here.

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:12.798
- I understand that.

00:17:12.798 --> 00:17:16.230
- But what I like about
that is that it seems like

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:20.250
we know the brain is a very
economical organ in some sense,

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:25.050
despite its high metabolic
demands and this variation

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:28.380
in perception from one
individual to the next, at once,

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:30.210
seems like a problem because

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:32.820
we're all literally
seeing different things.

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.860
And yet we function,

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:38.700
we function well enough for
most of us to avoid death

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:42.600
and cliffs and eating poisons and so forth

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.630
and to enjoy some aspects
of life one hopes.

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:49.770
So is there a general statement
that we can about the brain,

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:52.680
not just as a organ to
generate perception,

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.960
not just as an organ to keep us alive,

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:58.770
but also an organ that is trying to batch

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:01.620
our behaviors into general
categories of outcome?

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.750
- I think so, but, and again,

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:06.900
I think the world of Marcus too,

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:11.900
and I think he's right
that broadly speaking,

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:15.190
you could categorize a
lot of behaviors falling

00:18:16.321 --> 00:18:17.154
into those two categories

00:18:17.154 --> 00:18:20.010
and that's 100% likely

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:25.010
to be the case for
animals in the wild where

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:29.853
the choices are not necessarily binary,

00:18:31.136 --> 00:18:34.635
but they're very unique and distinct.

00:18:34.635 --> 00:18:37.380
Do I want to eat this?

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:39.123
Do I want to kill that?

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:41.220
Do I want to go there?

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:43.053
Or do I want to go here?

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:49.910
We humans deviated from
that world long ago.

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:56.350
And learned to experience life

00:18:57.810 --> 00:19:00.960
where we do things that
we should not be doing.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:02.340
- Some of us more than others.

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.070
- Exactly.

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:07.503
You know, in my own world of taste,

00:19:09.587 --> 00:19:11.610
the likelihood that an animal

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:15.280
in the wild will enjoy
eating something bitter

00:19:18.622 --> 00:19:22.810
it's inconceivable, yet
we love tonic water.

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:29.520
We enjoy, we like living on the edge.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:32.950
We love enjoying experiences

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:38.883
that makes us human.

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:45.783
And that goes beyond that
simple set of categories,

00:19:47.307 --> 00:19:51.930
which is yummy, yukky, ah, who cares.

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:54.210
And so I think it's not a bad palette,

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:59.210
but I think it's overly reductionist

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:04.350
for certainly what we humans do.

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:05.956
- I agree.

00:20:05.956 --> 00:20:06.789
And since we're here in New York,

00:20:06.789 --> 00:20:08.583
I can say that the many options,

00:20:10.322 --> 00:20:12.450
the extensive variety of food,

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:15.900
flora and fauna in New York explains

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:19.920
a lot of the more nuanced
behaviors that we observe.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:23.550
Let's talk about taste because although

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:25.830
you've done extensive work
in the field of vision,

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:29.310
and it's a topic that
I love you could spend

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:32.460
all day on taste is fascinating.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:33.600
First of all, I'd like to know

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:38.190
why you migrated from studying
vision to studying taste.

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.540
And perhaps in that description,

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:42.300
you could highlight to us
why we should think about

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:44.883
and how we should think
about this sense of taste.

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:48.270
- My goal has been to understand,

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:50.943
as I highlighted before how
the brain does its magic,

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:55.200
you know what part you
might wonder, ideally,

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:58.383
I like to help contribute
to understand all of it.

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:03.753
You know how do you encode
and decode emotions?

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.953
How do you encode and
decode memories and actions?

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:10.890
How do you make decisions?

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:14.640
How do you transform
detection into perception?

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:17.954
And the list goes on and on.

00:21:17.954 --> 00:21:22.954
But one of the key things
in science, as you know,

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:27.330
is ensuring that you always
ask the right question

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:31.323
so that you have a
possibility of answering it.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:36.240
Because if the question
cannot be tractable or reduced

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:40.470
to an experimental path
that helps you resolve it,

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:43.620
then we end up doing
some really fun science,

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:45.570
but not necessarily answering

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:48.333
the important problem
that we want to study.

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:51.048
Make sense?

00:21:51.048 --> 00:21:54.360
- From a first person perspective, yes.

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:56.527
The hardest question,

00:21:56.527 --> 00:21:59.130
the most important question
is what are you going

00:21:59.130 --> 00:21:59.963
to try and answer.

00:21:59.963 --> 00:22:01.470
- You got it.

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:02.430
And so for example

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:07.053
I will have to understand
the neural basis of empathy.

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:10.890
- [Andrew] There's a big market for that.

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:13.734
- 100%, but I wouldn't even know.

00:22:13.734 --> 00:22:16.580
I mean, at the molecular
level, that's what we do.

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:20.610
How do the circuits in your
brain create that sense?

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.430
I have no clue how to do it.

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:25.230
I can come up with ways to think about it,

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:27.960
but I like to understand what

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:31.923
in your brain makes someone
a great philanthropist.

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.793
What is the neural basis of love?

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:40.380
I wouldn't even know where to begin.

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:42.090
So if I want to begin to study

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.970
these questions about brain function,

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:48.810
that can cover so many
aspects of the brain,

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:52.953
I need to choose a problem
that affords me that window.

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:55.740
But in a way that I can ask questions

00:22:55.740 --> 00:23:00.210
that get me answers and among the senses

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:04.016
that have the capacity

00:23:04.016 --> 00:23:06.066
of transforming detection into perception

00:23:07.216 --> 00:23:11.040
of being stored as memories
or creating emotions,

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:15.840
of giving you different
actions and perceptions

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:18.360
as a function of the internal state.

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:19.230
You know, when you're hungry,

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:22.710
things taste very differently
that when you're sated,

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:24.093
how, why?

00:23:25.113 --> 00:23:26.100
When you taste something,

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.320
you now remember this amazing meal

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:31.443
you had with your first
date, how does that happen?

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:36.720
So if I want to begin to
explore all of these things

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.013
that the brain does,

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:44.120
I felt I have to choose a
sensory system that affords

00:23:45.135 --> 00:23:50.135
some degree of simplicity in the way

00:23:50.595 --> 00:23:54.990
that the input output
relationships are put together.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:58.050
And in a way that still can
be used to ask every one

00:23:58.050 --> 00:24:00.030
of these problems that the brain

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:04.564
has to ultimately compute,
encode and decode.

00:24:04.564 --> 00:24:07.230
And what's remarkable

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:08.763
about the taste system at the time

00:24:08.763 --> 00:24:10.840
that I began working on this

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:14.110
is that nothing was known

00:24:14.968 --> 00:24:17.313
about the molecular basis of taste.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:20.610
You know, we knew that we could taste

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:23.460
what has been usually defined as

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:27.990
the five basic taste
qualities, sweet, sour,

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:31.740
bitter, salty, and umami,

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:33.300
umami is a Japanese word

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:36.033
that means yummy, delicious.

00:24:37.037 --> 00:24:39.840
And that's nearly every animal species,

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:42.180
the taste of amino acids.

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:47.180
And in humans, it's mostly
associated with the taste of MSG,

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:50.850
monosodium glutamate, one
amino acid in particular.

00:24:50.850 --> 00:24:52.650
- What are just by way of example,

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:57.600
some foods that are rich in
the umami evoking stimulation?

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:02.600
- Seaweed, tomatoes, cheese,
eh, and it's a great,

00:25:03.510 --> 00:25:06.480
great flavor enhancer.

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:09.183
It enriches our sensory experience.

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:11.723
And so the beautiful thing of the system

00:25:11.723 --> 00:25:16.053
is that the lines of
input are limited to five.

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:20.157
You know, sweet, sour,
bitter, salt and umami,

00:25:20.157 --> 00:25:24.630
and each of them has a
predetermined meaning,

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:29.490
you are born liking sugar
and disliking bitter.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:31.530
You have no choice.

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:34.083
These are hard wire systems,

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:39.210
but of course you can
learn to dislike sugar

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:43.830
and to like bitters, but in the while,

00:25:43.830 --> 00:25:45.730
let's take humans out of the question,

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:50.550
these are a hundred percent predetermined.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:55.550
You are born with that specific
valance value for each taste

00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:02.450
of sweet, umami and low salt
are attractive taste qualities.

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:09.003
They evoke appetitive responses,
I want to consume them.

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:14.880
And bitter and sour are innately,

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:18.090
predetermined to be aversive.

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:19.500
- Could I interrupt you just briefly

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:22.150
and ask a question about that exact point

00:26:23.405 --> 00:26:25.505
for something to be appetitive to and some

00:26:27.591 --> 00:26:30.570
other taste to be aversive
and for those to be hardwired,

00:26:30.570 --> 00:26:35.340
can we assume that the
sensation of very bitter

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:37.170
or of activation of bitter receptors

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:40.770
in the mouth activates a neural circuit

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:43.350
that causes closing of the mouth,

