in a way that can reexperience the world

00:41:01.860 --> 00:41:04.980
in the right way and
there are other organs

00:41:04.980 --> 00:41:08.163
that have the same underlying logic.

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:11.610
Your gut, your intestines
are the same way.

00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:12.660
Amazing.

00:41:12.660 --> 00:41:16.590
Again, they're receiving
everything that you ingest.

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:21.058
God forbid what's there, from the spiciest

00:41:21.058 --> 00:41:25.860
to the most horrible tastings
or the most delicious.

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:26.880
And again,

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:31.200
those intestinal cells
whose role is to ultimately

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:34.980
take all these nutrients and
bring them into the body.

00:41:34.980 --> 00:41:39.980
Also renew in a very, very fast cycle,

00:41:42.868 --> 00:41:46.263
olfactory neurons in
your nose is the example.

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:49.830
So then A, yes,

00:41:49.830 --> 00:41:54.720
you're burning a lot of your
cells and is over for those.

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:57.510
The good news is that
they're going to come back,

00:41:57.510 --> 00:42:00.694
but we know that when you
burn yourself with tea,

00:42:00.694 --> 00:42:03.514
they come back within 20 minutes,

00:42:03.514 --> 00:42:05.040
30 minutes, an hour,

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:09.570
and these cells are not
renewing in that timeframe.

00:42:09.570 --> 00:42:11.550
They're not listening to your needs.

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:13.683
They have their own internal clock.

00:42:15.240 --> 00:42:20.240
And so you are really affecting,

00:42:22.020 --> 00:42:25.533
you're damaging them in a
way that they can recover.

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:27.810
And then they come back

00:42:27.810 --> 00:42:30.420
and you also damage your
somatosensory cells.

00:42:30.420 --> 00:42:35.373
These are the results that
feel things, not taste things.

00:42:36.342 --> 00:42:39.810
And then you wait half an hour or so,

00:42:39.810 --> 00:42:44.550
and then my goodness, thank
God, it's back to normal.

00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:45.480
- And most of the time,

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:48.157
I don't even notice the
transition realizing,

00:42:48.157 --> 00:42:50.403
as you tell me, and I'll later,

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:54.750
I'll ask you about the relationship
between odor and taste.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:58.920
But as a next step, along this circuit,

00:42:58.920 --> 00:43:01.860
let's assume I ingest
some, let's keep it simple.

00:43:01.860 --> 00:43:03.270
A sweet taste.

00:43:03.270 --> 00:43:06.603
- Let's make it even simpler.

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:11.490
But at the same time,
perhaps more informative,

00:43:11.490 --> 00:43:15.090
let's compare and
contrast sweet and bitter

00:43:15.090 --> 00:43:18.153
as we follow their lines
from the tongue to the brain.

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:21.353
So the first thing is that

00:43:21.353 --> 00:43:25.140
the two evoke diametrically
opposed behaviors.

00:43:25.140 --> 00:43:28.350
If we have to come up with
two sensory experience

00:43:28.350 --> 00:43:29.940
that represent polar opposites,

00:43:29.940 --> 00:43:31.350
it will be sweet and video.

00:43:31.350 --> 00:43:33.750
There are not two colors that
represent polar opposites

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:35.730
because you could say black and white,

00:43:35.730 --> 00:43:36.780
they are polar opposites,

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:38.130
one detects only one thing.

00:43:39.046 --> 00:43:40.620
The other one detects everything,

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:44.970
but they don't evoke different behaviors.

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:47.523
- Even political parties
have some overlap.

00:43:48.660 --> 00:43:51.030
- Sweet and bitter are
the two opposite ends

00:43:51.030 --> 00:43:52.533
of the sensory spectrum,

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:58.610
now, a taste can be
defined by two features.

00:44:00.355 --> 00:44:02.610
Again, I'm a reductionist,

00:44:02.610 --> 00:44:05.400
so I'm reducing it in a way that I think

00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:07.710
it's easier to follow the signal.

00:44:07.710 --> 00:44:12.710
And the two features are
its quality and its valance

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:16.200
and valance with a little V

00:44:16.200 --> 00:44:18.693
that's what we say in Spanish, with a V,

00:44:20.700 --> 00:44:25.700
means the value of that experience, right?

00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:31.593
Or in this case of that
stimuli and you take sweet,

00:44:32.460 --> 00:44:36.630
sweet has a quality, an identity,

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:38.430
and that's what you and I will refer

00:44:38.430 --> 00:44:40.530
to as the taste of sweet.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:42.573
We know exactly what it tastes like,

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:47.613
But sweet also has a positive valance,

00:44:49.530 --> 00:44:53.310
which makes it incredibly
attractive and appetitive,

00:44:53.310 --> 00:44:55.410
but it's attractive and appetitive,

00:44:55.410 --> 00:44:57.700
as I'll tell you in a second independent

00:44:58.967 --> 00:45:00.810
of its identity and quality.

00:45:00.810 --> 00:45:01.890
In fact, we have been able

00:45:01.890 --> 00:45:05.250
to engineer animals where we completely

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:08.190
remove the valance from the stimuli.

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:10.860
So these animals can taste sweet,

00:45:10.860 --> 00:45:13.263
can recognize it as sweet,

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:16.710
but it's no longer attractive.

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:19.563
It's just one more chemical stimuli.

00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:24.390
And that's because the
identity and the valence

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:27.243
are encoded in two separate
parts of the brain.

00:45:28.650 --> 00:45:30.990
In the case of bitter,

00:45:30.990 --> 00:45:34.012
again, it has on the
one hand its identity,

00:45:34.012 --> 00:45:37.383
its quality, and you know
exactly what bitter tastes like.

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:41.460
- I can taste it now,
even as you describe it.

00:45:41.460 --> 00:45:43.770
- But it also has a valence and that's

00:45:43.770 --> 00:45:48.753
a negative valance because
it evokes aversive behaviors.

00:45:50.100 --> 00:45:50.933
Are we on?

00:45:50.933 --> 00:45:51.810
- Absolutely.

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:52.643
And it comes to mind.

00:45:52.643 --> 00:45:54.120
I remember telling some kids recently

00:45:54.120 --> 00:45:55.950
that we're going to go get ice
cream and it was interesting.

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:58.311
They looked up and they started smacking

00:45:58.311 --> 00:45:59.610
their like, they'll actually.

00:45:59.610 --> 00:46:03.060
- The anticipatory response.

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:03.903
Absolutely.

00:46:04.860 --> 00:46:07.920
When we talk about the gut
brain maybe we'll get there.

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:10.890
So then the signals, if we
follow now, these two lines,

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:13.140
they're really like two separate keys

00:46:13.140 --> 00:46:15.363
at the two ends of this keyboard.

00:46:16.710 --> 00:46:21.210
And you press one key and
you activate this cord.

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:25.140
So you activate the sweet cells
throughout your oral cavity.

00:46:25.140 --> 00:46:29.310
And they all converge into
a group of sweet neurons

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:34.310
in the next station, which
is still outside the brain,

00:46:34.650 --> 00:46:37.083
it's one of the taste ganglia.

00:46:38.160 --> 00:46:41.503
These are the neurons
that innervate your tongue

00:46:41.503 --> 00:46:42.600
and the oral cavity.

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:44.130
- Where do they sit approximately?

00:46:44.130 --> 00:46:46.110
- Around there.
- Right here around there.

00:46:46.110 --> 00:46:47.250
The lymph nodes, more or less?

00:46:47.250 --> 00:46:48.083
- You got it.

00:46:49.958 --> 00:46:52.650
And there are two main
ganglia that innervate

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:59.150
the vast majority of all
taste buds in the oral cavity.

00:46:59.280 --> 00:47:02.580
And then from there that sweet signal

00:47:02.580 --> 00:47:04.800
goes onto the brain stem.

00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:08.943
The brain stem is the entry
of the body into the brain.

00:47:10.080 --> 00:47:12.960
And there are different
areas of the brain stem.

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:15.780
And there are different groups
of neurons in the brain stem.

00:47:15.780 --> 00:47:18.453
And there's this unique area in a unique,

00:47:21.711 --> 00:47:25.467
a topographically defined
location in the rostral side

00:47:28.260 --> 00:47:32.700
of the brain that receives
all of the taste input.

00:47:32.700 --> 00:47:34.513
- A very dense area of the brain.

00:47:34.513 --> 00:47:37.800
- A very rich area of the brain.

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:39.030
Exactly.

00:47:39.030 --> 00:47:40.508
And from there,

00:47:40.508 --> 00:47:42.810
the sweet signal goes to this other area

00:47:42.810 --> 00:47:45.603
higher up on the brain stem.

00:47:46.950 --> 00:47:51.210
And then it goes through
a number of stations where

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:55.440
that sweet signal goes from
sweet neuron to sweet neuron,

00:47:55.440 --> 00:47:59.433
to sweet neuron, to
eventually get to your cortex.

00:48:00.300 --> 00:48:03.180
And once it gets to your taste cortex,

00:48:03.180 --> 00:48:08.180
that's where meaning is
imposed into that signal.

00:48:08.580 --> 00:48:13.110
It's then, and only then this is what

00:48:13.110 --> 00:48:16.170
the data suggests that now you

00:48:16.170 --> 00:48:20.760
can identify this as a sweet stimuli.

00:48:20.760 --> 00:48:23.160
- [Andrew] And how quickly
does that all happen?

00:48:24.399 --> 00:48:29.399
- You know, the time scale of
the nervous system it's fast.

00:48:29.832 --> 00:48:32.070
- So within less than a second.

00:48:32.070 --> 00:48:34.409
- [Charles] Yeah, absolutely.

00:48:34.409 --> 00:48:36.480
- I rarely mistake bitter for sweet.

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:39.480
Maybe with respect to people
and my own poor judgment,

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:42.123
but not with respect to taste.

00:48:43.110 --> 00:48:43.943
- And in fact,

00:48:44.803 --> 00:48:46.830
we can demonstrate this
because we can stick electrodes

00:48:46.830 --> 00:48:50.007
at each of these stations, conceptually,

00:48:50.007 --> 00:48:51.960
and we can stimulate the tongue,

00:48:51.960 --> 00:48:54.570
and then we can record
the signals pretty much

00:48:54.570 --> 00:48:57.420
time log the stimulus delivery.

00:48:57.420 --> 00:49:00.393
You deliver the stimuli and
within a fraction of a second,

00:49:01.500 --> 00:49:05.730
you see now the response
in this following stations,

00:49:05.730 --> 00:49:08.100
now it gets to the cortex,

00:49:08.100 --> 00:49:12.630
and now in there you impose
meaning to that taste.

00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:17.100
There is an area of your
brain that represents

00:49:17.100 --> 00:49:20.490
the taste of sweet in taste cortex

00:49:20.490 --> 00:49:25.263
and a different area that
represents the taste of bitter.

00:49:26.100 --> 00:49:28.120
In a sense, there is a topographic map

00:49:29.070 --> 00:49:31.383
of these taste qualities
inside your brain.

00:49:33.090 --> 00:49:35.193
Now we're going to do
a thought experiment.

00:49:36.270 --> 00:49:37.950
Now, if this group of neurons

00:49:37.950 --> 00:49:40.890
in your cortex really represents

00:49:40.890 --> 00:49:44.340
the sense of sweet and
this added different group

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:49.320
of neurons in your brain
represents the taste,

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:52.417
the perception of bitter,

00:49:52.417 --> 00:49:55.830
then we should be able to do two things.

00:49:55.830 --> 00:49:58.770
First, I should be able
to go into your brain,

00:49:58.770 --> 00:50:03.000
somehow silence those neurons,

00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:06.603
find a way to prevent
them from being activated.

00:50:09.216 --> 00:50:10.049
And I can give you all the sweet you want,

00:50:10.049 --> 00:50:12.363
and you'll never know
that you're tasting sweet.

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:18.300
And conversely, I should be
able to go into your brain,

00:50:18.300 --> 00:50:21.750
come up with a way to
activate those neurons

00:50:21.750 --> 00:50:25.170
while I'm giving you absolutely nothing.

00:50:25.170 --> 00:50:28.020
And you're going to think
that you are getting

00:50:28.020 --> 00:50:33.020
that full percept and that's
precisely what we have done.

00:50:34.080 --> 00:50:36.750
And that's precisely what you get.

00:50:36.750 --> 00:50:39.240
This of course is in the brain of mice.

00:50:39.240 --> 00:50:41.670
- But presumably in humans
it would work similarly.

00:50:41.670 --> 00:50:44.190
- Absolutely the same, zero doubt.

00:50:44.190 --> 00:50:45.570
I have no questions.

00:50:45.570 --> 00:50:50.100
So this attests to two important things,

00:50:50.100 --> 00:50:54.093
the first to the predetermined
nature of the sense of taste,

00:50:55.560 --> 00:50:57.600
because it means I can go to these parts

00:50:57.600 --> 00:51:01.200
of your brain in the
absence of any stimuli.

00:51:01.200 --> 00:51:06.120
And have you throw the
full behavioral experience.

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:08.430
In fact, when we activate in your cortex,

00:51:08.430 --> 00:51:11.823
these bitter neurons, the
animal can start gagging,

00:51:14.160 --> 00:51:16.713
but it's drinking only water,

00:51:18.390 --> 00:51:23.390
but the animal thinks that's
getting a bitter stimuli.

00:51:24.112 --> 00:51:25.110
[audience applauds] This is amazing.

00:51:25.110 --> 00:51:27.197
- And so, and the second,

00:51:27.197 --> 00:51:28.030
just to finish the line so

00:51:28.030 --> 00:51:30.210
that it doesn't sound
like it teaches two things

00:51:30.210 --> 00:51:32.010
and then I only give you one lesson.

00:51:36.342 --> 00:51:38.320
It is that it substantiates

00:51:40.372 --> 00:51:42.542
this capacity of the brain to segregate,

00:51:42.542 --> 00:51:46.170
to separate in this nodes of action,

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:47.880
the representation of these

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:51.033
two diametrically opposed percepts.

00:51:51.930 --> 00:51:54.080
Which is sweet, for
example, versus bitter.

00:51:54.990 --> 00:51:56.250
- The reason I say amazing,

00:51:56.250 --> 00:51:59.463
and that is also amazing is the following,

00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:02.790
you told us earlier, and
you're absolutely correct,

00:52:02.790 --> 00:52:05.940
of course, that the end of the day,

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:07.860
whether or not it's one
group of neurons over here,

00:52:07.860 --> 00:52:10.350
and another group of neurons over there,

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:12.150
which is the way it turns out to be,

00:52:13.080 --> 00:52:15.660
electrical activity is the generic,

00:52:15.660 --> 00:52:18.153
common language of both sets of neurons.

00:52:19.638 --> 00:52:20.471
So that raises the question for me,

00:52:20.471 --> 00:52:23.190
of whether or not those
separate sets of neurons

00:52:23.190 --> 00:52:24.960
are connected to areas of the brain

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:28.590
that create this sense
of valence or whether

00:52:28.590 --> 00:52:31.860
or not they're simply
created connected, excuse me,

00:52:31.860 --> 00:52:34.950
to sets of neurons that evoke
distinct behaviors of moving

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:37.950
towards and inhaling more
and licking or aversive are

00:52:37.950 --> 00:52:40.440
we essentially interpreting our behavior

00:52:40.440 --> 00:52:44.100
and our micro responses
or are micro responses

00:52:44.100 --> 00:52:46.380
and our behaviors the
consequence of the percept?

00:52:46.380 --> 00:52:48.633
- Excellent, excellent question.

00:52:49.736 --> 00:52:53.917
So first answer is they go into

00:52:54.895 --> 00:52:59.080
an area of the brain
where valence is imposed,

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:05.240
and that area is known as the
amygdala and the sweet neurons

00:53:05.340 --> 00:53:08.670
go to a different area
than the bitter neurons.

00:53:08.670 --> 00:53:10.560
Now I want to do a thought experiment

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:12.750
because I think your audience
might appreciate this.

00:53:12.750 --> 00:53:16.590
Let's say I activate this group of neurons

00:53:16.590 --> 00:53:18.610
and the animal increases licking

00:53:19.950 --> 00:53:22.080
and I'm activating the sweet neurons.

00:53:22.080 --> 00:53:25.500
And so that's expected
because now it's tasting

00:53:25.500 --> 00:53:29.407
this water as if it was sugar.

00:53:29.407 --> 00:53:32.130
Now this Moses transforming
water into wine

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:34.290
in this case we're going
to, and today's Passover,

00:53:34.290 --> 00:53:37.533
so this is an appropriate example,

00:53:38.700 --> 00:53:42.033
we're transforming it into sweet.

00:53:44.614 --> 00:53:48.360
But how do I know that
activating them is evoking

00:53:48.360 --> 00:53:53.360
a positive feeling inside,
a goodness, a satisfaction,

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:58.430
or I love it versus I'm
just increasing licking,

00:53:58.517 --> 00:54:00.300
which is the other option,

00:54:00.300 --> 00:54:02.790
because all we're seeing is
that the animal is leaking more.

00:54:02.790 --> 00:54:05.670
And we're trying to infer that that means

00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:06.870
that he's feeling something

00:54:06.870 --> 00:54:08.940
really good versus you know what,

00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:11.730
that piano line is going back
straight into the tongue.

00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:14.883
And all it's doing is
forcing it to move faster.

00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:18.000
Well, we can actually separate this

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:20.220
by doing experiments that allows

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:22.000
to fundamentally distinguish them

00:54:24.030 --> 00:54:26.070
and imagine the following experiment,

00:54:26.070 --> 00:54:27.870
I'm going to take the
animal and I'm going to

00:54:27.870 --> 00:54:31.593
put them inside a box that has two sides.

00:54:32.730 --> 00:54:35.520
And the two sides have features
that make him different.

00:54:35.520 --> 00:54:38.910
One has yellow little toys.

00:54:38.910 --> 00:54:41.340
The other one has green toys.

00:54:41.340 --> 00:54:43.740
One has little black stripes.

00:54:43.740 --> 00:54:45.330
The other one has blue stripes.

00:54:45.330 --> 00:54:49.230
So the animal can tell the
two halves, I take the mouse,

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:53.610
put them inside this
arena, this play arena.

00:54:53.610 --> 00:54:55.620
And it will explore and potter

00:54:55.620 --> 00:54:58.893
around both sides with equal frequency.

00:55:00.198 --> 00:55:01.615
And now what I'm going to do

00:55:01.615 --> 00:55:03.630
is I'm going to activate these neurons,

00:55:03.630 --> 00:55:05.013
these sweet neurons,

00:55:06.060 --> 00:55:07.140
every time the animal

00:55:07.140 --> 00:55:09.723
is on the side with the yellow stripes.

00:55:16.051 --> 00:55:20.193
And if that is creating a
positive internal state,

00:55:21.090 --> 00:55:24.060
what would the animal now want to do?

00:55:24.060 --> 00:55:29.060
It will want to stay on the
side with the yellow stripes.

00:55:30.090 --> 00:55:31.808
There's no licking here.

00:55:31.808 --> 00:55:33.330
The animal is not extending its tongue

00:55:33.330 --> 00:55:35.313
every time I'm activating this neurons.

00:55:36.360 --> 00:55:39.423
This is known as a place preference test.

00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:42.370
And it's generally used,

00:55:42.370 --> 00:55:47.030
it's just one form of many
different tests to demonstrate

00:55:48.690 --> 00:55:52.590
that the activation of a group

00:55:52.590 --> 00:55:55.620
of neurons in the brain is imposing,

00:55:55.620 --> 00:55:59.790
for example, a positive
versus a negative valance.

00:55:59.790 --> 00:56:01.560
Whereas if I do the same thing

00:56:01.560 --> 00:56:03.420
by activating the bitter neurons,

00:56:03.420 --> 00:56:06.750
the animal will actively
want now to stay away

00:56:06.750 --> 00:56:11.750
from the side where these
neurons are being activated.

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:14.330
- And that's precisely what you see.

00:56:14.330 --> 00:56:16.410
- [Charles] And that's
precisely what we see.

00:56:16.410 --> 00:56:17.880
- Many people, including myself,

00:56:17.880 --> 00:56:19.290
are familiar with the experience

00:56:19.290 --> 00:56:22.803
of going to a restaurant,
eating a variety of foods.

00:56:24.167 --> 00:56:25.260
And then fortunately
doesn't happen that often,

00:56:25.260 --> 00:56:27.600
but then feeling very sick.

00:56:27.600 --> 00:56:29.550
I learned coming up in neuroscience

00:56:29.550 --> 00:56:34.530
that this is one strong
example of one trial learning,

00:56:34.530 --> 00:56:37.290
that from that point on,

00:56:37.290 --> 00:56:41.040
it's not the restaurant
or the waitress or waiter,

00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:42.450
or the date,

00:56:42.450 --> 00:56:47.220
but it's my notion of it
had to have been the shrimp

00:56:47.220 --> 00:56:50.280
that leads me to then want to
avoid shrimp in every context,

00:56:50.280 --> 00:56:52.050
maybe even shrimp powder.

00:56:52.050 --> 00:56:52.950
- You got it.

00:56:52.950 --> 00:56:54.390
- For a very long time.

00:56:54.390 --> 00:56:56.790
I can imagine all the evolutionarily

00:56:56.790 --> 00:57:00.390
adaptive reasons why this
such a phenomenon would exist.

00:57:00.390 --> 00:57:04.049
Do we have any concept of where
in this pathway it exists?

00:57:04.049 --> 00:57:06.700
- We do, we know actually
a significant amount

00:57:07.994 --> 00:57:11.100
at a general level.

00:57:11.100 --> 00:57:12.840
In fact, more than shrimp,

00:57:12.840 --> 00:57:15.810
oysters are even a more dramatic example,

00:57:15.810 --> 00:57:20.130
one bad oyster is all
you need to be driven

00:57:20.130 --> 00:57:22.053
that way for the next six months.

00:57:22.993 --> 00:57:24.540
- I think because the
texture alone is something

00:57:24.540 --> 00:57:25.710
that one learns to overcome.

00:57:25.710 --> 00:57:27.630
I actually really enjoy oysters.

00:57:27.630 --> 00:57:32.520
I despise mussels, despise
shrimp, not the animal,

00:57:32.520 --> 00:57:35.280
but the taste and yet
oysters, for some reason,

00:57:35.280 --> 00:57:36.930
I've yet to have a bad experience.

00:57:36.930 --> 00:57:38.834
- It's like, uni, by the way

00:57:38.834 --> 00:57:42.150
texture is hard to get
over, but once you get over,

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:43.230
it's delicious.

00:57:43.230 --> 00:57:44.820
- That's what they tell me.

00:57:44.820 --> 00:57:46.530
We were both in San Diego at one point,

00:57:46.530 --> 00:57:48.720
and I'll give a plug to Sushi Ota,

00:57:48.720 --> 00:57:51.047
is kind of the famous place,

00:57:51.047 --> 00:57:53.280
and they have amazing uni
and I've tried it twice.

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:55.353
And I'm oh for two.

00:57:56.206 --> 00:57:57.690
It somehow the texture outweighs

00:57:57.690 --> 00:58:00.585
any kind of the deliciousness
that people report.

00:58:00.585 --> 00:58:02.100
- It's a very acquired taste.

00:58:02.100 --> 00:58:05.790
It's like beer, I grew up in Chile,

00:58:05.790 --> 00:58:08.640
that's where the accent comes
from in case anyone wonder.

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:12.360
And by the time I came
here to graduate school,

00:58:12.360 --> 00:58:17.360
I was 19, too old to overcome
my heavy Chilian accent.

00:58:21.327 --> 00:58:22.590
So here I am, 40 years, 50 years late,

00:58:22.590 --> 00:58:23.979
not quite 40 plus-

00:58:23.979 --> 00:58:24.812
- We appreciate it.

00:58:24.812 --> 00:58:28.113
- And I still sound like
I just came off the boat.

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:31.680
So in Chile, you don't drink
beer when you're young,

00:58:31.680 --> 00:58:32.820
you drink wine.

00:58:32.820 --> 00:58:34.623
Chili's a huge wine producer.

00:58:36.030 --> 00:58:38.133
So when I came to the US,

00:58:40.144 --> 00:58:42.857
all of my classmates were
drinking beer because

00:58:44.543 --> 00:58:46.650
they had finished college
where they were all

00:58:46.650 --> 00:58:49.560
you're drinking and graduate school,

00:58:49.560 --> 00:58:52.890
you're working 18 hours a day every day,

00:58:52.890 --> 00:58:55.980
the way they relax, let's
go and have some beers.

00:58:55.980 --> 00:58:57.060
- And beer is cheaper.

00:58:57.060 --> 00:58:58.770
- And beer is cheap,

00:58:58.770 --> 00:59:00.983
and we were being clearly
underpaid may I add.

00:59:04.530 --> 00:59:05.670
I couldn't do it.

00:59:05.670 --> 00:59:07.530
It's an acquired taste.

00:59:07.530 --> 00:59:09.570
It was too late by then.

00:59:09.570 --> 00:59:12.630
And here I am 60 plus.

00:59:12.630 --> 00:59:16.110
And if you take all the beer
I've drank in my entire life,

00:59:16.110 --> 00:59:18.760
I would say they add to less than

00:59:19.643 --> 00:59:21.390
an eight ounce glass of water.

00:59:21.390 --> 00:59:22.223
- Impressive.

00:59:22.223 --> 00:59:24.000
Well, your health is
probably better for it.

00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:25.053
- I'm not sure.

00:59:26.010 --> 00:59:27.300
- Your physical health anyway.

00:59:27.300 --> 00:59:30.540
- So it goes back to acquired taste.

00:59:30.540 --> 00:59:33.213
This is the connection
to uni and to oysters.

00:59:34.912 --> 00:59:36.570
Now, going back to the one trial learning

00:59:36.570 --> 00:59:38.850
this is the great thing about our brains,

00:59:38.850 --> 00:59:40.500
certain things we need to repeat

00:59:40.500 --> 00:59:44.552
a hundred times to learn
them, hello operator.

00:59:44.552 --> 00:59:47.190
Can I have the phone number
for sushi Ota, please?

00:59:47.190 --> 00:59:49.470
And then she'll give it
to you over the phone,

00:59:49.470 --> 00:59:51.270
at least in the old days.

00:59:51.270 --> 00:59:54.420
And then you need to
repeat it to yourself over

00:59:54.420 --> 00:59:56.894
and over and over, over the next minute.

00:59:56.894 --> 00:59:58.194
So you can dial sushi Ota,

00:59:59.070 --> 01:00:03.150
and five minutes later, it's gone.

01:00:03.150 --> 01:00:05.343
That's what we call working memory.

01:00:06.570 --> 01:00:08.520
Then there is the short term memory.

01:00:08.520 --> 01:00:10.650
We park our car.

01:00:10.650 --> 01:00:12.210
And if we're lucky, by the end of the day,

01:00:12.210 --> 01:00:13.683
we remember where it is.

01:00:15.060 --> 01:00:16.740
And then there is the long term memory.

01:00:16.740 --> 01:00:18.660
We remember the birthdays of every one

01:00:18.660 --> 01:00:21.573
of our children for the rest of our lives.

01:00:22.680 --> 01:00:26.230
Well, there are events that a
single event is so traumatic

01:00:28.170 --> 01:00:30.453
that it activates the circuits in a way

01:00:30.453 --> 01:00:33.000
that it's a one trial learning.

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:36.480
And the taste system is literally

01:00:36.480 --> 01:00:38.343
at the top of that food chain.

01:00:39.300 --> 01:00:40.850
And there is a phenomenon known

01:00:42.730 --> 01:00:44.460
as conditioned taste aversion.

01:00:44.460 --> 01:00:49.460
You can pair an attractive
stimuli with a really bad one,

01:00:53.011 --> 01:00:55.350
and you can make an animal begin

01:00:55.350 --> 01:00:58.503
to vehemently dislike for example, sugar.

01:01:00.030 --> 01:01:03.000
And that's because you've
conditioned the animals

01:01:03.000 --> 01:01:08.000
to now be averse to these
otherwise nice taste

01:01:08.520 --> 01:01:11.700
because it's been associated with malaise.

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:12.720
And when you do that,

01:01:12.720 --> 01:01:17.460
now you could begin to ask
why has change in the signal

01:01:17.460 --> 01:01:19.500
as it travels from the tongue

01:01:19.500 --> 01:01:22.650
to the brain in a normal animal

01:01:22.650 --> 01:01:26.160
versus an animal where you
have now transformed sweet

01:01:26.160 --> 01:01:28.920
from being attractive to being immersive.

01:01:28.920 --> 01:01:31.560
And this is the way now
you begin to explore

01:01:31.560 --> 01:01:36.480
how the brain changes,
the nature, the quality,

01:01:36.480 --> 01:01:41.480
the meaning of a stimuli
as a function of its state.

01:01:43.710 --> 01:01:46.293
- I have a number of
questions related to that,

01:01:48.060 --> 01:01:51.183
all of which relate to
this idea of context,

01:01:52.680 --> 01:01:54.930
because you mentioned before
that flavor is distinct

01:01:54.930 --> 01:01:56.790
from taste because flavor involves smell,

01:01:56.790 --> 01:01:59.305
texture, temperature,
and some other features,

01:01:59.305 --> 01:02:02.433
uni, sea urchin being a good example of,

01:02:03.630 --> 01:02:05.340
I can sense the texture.

01:02:05.340 --> 01:02:07.440
It actually it's, nah, I won't describe

01:02:07.440 --> 01:02:11.403
what it reminds me of,
for various reasons.

01:02:12.750 --> 01:02:15.480
The ability to place context into,

01:02:15.480 --> 01:02:17.550
to insert context into a perception,

01:02:17.550 --> 01:02:19.590
or rather to insert a perception

01:02:19.590 --> 01:02:22.083
into context is so powerful.

01:02:24.344 --> 01:02:27.300
And there's an element of
kind of mystery about it.

01:02:27.300 --> 01:02:30.810
But if we start to think
about some of the more nuance

01:02:30.810 --> 01:02:33.153
that we like to live at
the edge, as you say,

01:02:35.220 --> 01:02:39.390
how many different
tastes on the taste dial

01:02:39.390 --> 01:02:41.700
to go back to your analogy
earlier, the color dial,

01:02:41.700 --> 01:02:43.500
do you think that there
could be for something

01:02:43.500 --> 01:02:46.530
as fixed as bitter, so for instance,

01:02:46.530 --> 01:02:49.440
I don't think I like bitter taste,

01:02:49.440 --> 01:02:52.650
but I like some fermented foods
that seem to have a little

01:02:52.650 --> 01:02:53.970
bit of sour and have a little bit

01:02:53.970 --> 01:02:56.700
of that briny flavor.

01:02:56.700 --> 01:02:58.620
How much plasticity do
you think there is there

01:02:58.620 --> 01:03:01.050
and in particular across the lifespan,

01:03:01.050 --> 01:03:03.000
because I think one of
the most salient examples

01:03:03.000 --> 01:03:07.560
of this is that kids don't seem
to like certain vegetables,

01:03:07.560 --> 01:03:11.250
but they all are hardwired
to like sweet tastes.

01:03:11.250 --> 01:03:13.800
And yet you could also imagine
that one of the reasons

01:03:13.800 --> 01:03:17.790
why they may eventually grow
to incorporate vegetables is

01:03:17.790 --> 01:03:20.010
because of some knowledge
that vegetables might be-

01:03:20.010 --> 01:03:21.760
- Good for you.
- Better for them.

01:03:22.823 --> 01:03:26.430
So is there a change in the
receptors, the distribution,

01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:28.170
the number, the sensitivity, et cetera,

01:03:28.170 --> 01:03:30.240
that can explain the transition

01:03:30.240 --> 01:03:32.190
from wanting to avoid vegetables,

01:03:32.190 --> 01:03:33.890
to being willing to eat vegetables

01:03:34.924 --> 01:03:37.500
simply in childhood to early development.

01:03:37.500 --> 01:03:40.076
Yes, so I going to take the
question slightly differently,

01:03:40.076 --> 01:03:41.880
but I think it would illustrate the point.

01:03:41.880 --> 01:03:43.390
And I want to just

01:03:44.565 --> 01:03:48.660
use the difference between
the olfactory system

01:03:48.660 --> 01:03:50.793
and the taste system to make the point,

01:03:52.410 --> 01:03:54.750
taste system five basic pallets,

01:03:54.750 --> 01:03:56.400
sweet, sour, bitter, salt, umami,

01:03:56.400 --> 01:03:58.710
each of them has a predetermined identity.

01:03:58.710 --> 01:04:01.159
We know exactly what and valence.

01:04:01.159 --> 01:04:02.730
These are attractive.

01:04:02.730 --> 01:04:04.500
These are aversive.

01:04:04.500 --> 01:04:05.820
In the olfactory system,

01:04:05.820 --> 01:04:07.890
it's claimed that we can smell millions

01:04:07.890 --> 01:04:12.890
of different odors, yet for the most part,

01:04:14.070 --> 01:04:19.070
none of them have an innate
predetermined meaning,

01:04:19.222 --> 01:04:21.990
in olfactory system,

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:26.130
meaning is imposed by
learning and experience.

01:04:26.130 --> 01:04:28.268
- Even the smell of smoke?

01:04:28.268 --> 01:04:29.101
- So I'm going to give you,

01:04:29.101 --> 01:04:30.630
I'm going to make it differently.

01:04:30.630 --> 01:04:33.930
They are a handful of the millions

01:04:33.930 --> 01:04:35.700
of odors that were claimed that,

01:04:36.543 --> 01:04:39.060
that you could immediately tell me these

01:04:39.060 --> 01:04:41.769
are adversive and these are attractive.

01:04:41.769 --> 01:04:43.393
- Vomit.

01:04:43.393 --> 01:04:46.726
- So vomit, it's not correct

01:04:46.726 --> 01:04:48.780
because I can assure you that they're

01:04:48.780 --> 01:04:52.680
cultures and societies where
things which are far less

01:04:52.680 --> 01:04:56.875
appealing than vomit do not
evoke a adverse reaction.

01:04:56.875 --> 01:04:57.866
- Really?

01:04:57.866 --> 01:05:00.120
- Really, sulfur would
be maybe a universal.

01:05:00.120 --> 01:05:01.470
I'm not talking pheromones.

01:05:02.571 --> 01:05:04.231
pheromones are in a different category

01:05:04.231 --> 01:05:06.660
that trigger innate responses,

01:05:06.660 --> 01:05:11.660
but nearly every other is afforded meaning

01:05:12.480 --> 01:05:14.280
by learning and experience.

01:05:14.280 --> 01:05:16.560
And that's why you like broccoli.

01:05:16.560 --> 01:05:19.440
And I despise broccoli because I remember

01:05:19.440 --> 01:05:23.132
my mother forcing me to eat broccoli.

01:05:23.132 --> 01:05:24.073
[audience applauds] I'm so sorry.

01:05:24.073 --> 01:05:25.373
- Same sensory experience.

01:05:28.080 --> 01:05:31.745
This accommodates two important things.

01:05:31.745 --> 01:05:33.373
In the case of taste,

01:05:33.373 --> 01:05:35.190
you have neurons at every
station that are for sweet,

01:05:35.190 --> 01:05:37.650
for sour, for bitter,
for salty, and umami,

01:05:37.650 --> 01:05:38.910
it's only five classes.

01:05:38.910 --> 01:05:41.130
So it's not going to
take a lot of your brain.

01:05:41.130 --> 01:05:44.100
If we can, in fact,
smell a million others,

01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:46.500
and everyone else of others
had to have predetermined,

01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:49.083
meaning there's not
going to be enough brain

01:05:49.083 --> 01:05:52.653
just to accommodate that one sense.

01:05:53.760 --> 01:05:57.363
And so evolution in its infinite wisdom,

01:05:58.440 --> 01:06:02.230
evolved a system where you put together

01:06:03.395 --> 01:06:06.407
a pathway and a cortex, olfactory cortex,

01:06:06.407 --> 01:06:11.407
where you have the capacity
to associate every other

01:06:13.800 --> 01:06:17.373
in a specific context that
now gives it the meaning.

01:06:19.320 --> 01:06:23.850
Now let's go back to the
original question then.

01:06:23.850 --> 01:06:28.200
So other than clearly
plastic, mega plastic,

01:06:28.200 --> 01:06:33.200
because it's fundamental
basis and neural organization,

01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:34.553
but taste,

01:06:34.553 --> 01:06:37.380
we just told you that
predetermined, hardwired,

01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:39.667
but predetermined, hardwired doesn't mean

01:06:39.667 --> 01:06:43.380
that it's not modulated
by learning or experience.

01:06:43.380 --> 01:06:47.640
It only means that you
are born liking sweet

01:06:47.640 --> 01:06:49.263
and disliking bitter.

01:06:50.670 --> 01:06:54.060
And we have many examples of plasticity,

01:06:54.060 --> 01:06:56.250
beer being one example.

01:06:56.250 --> 01:07:00.000
So why do we learn to
love beer and coffee.

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:05.000
It's because it has an
associated gain to the system

01:07:07.350 --> 01:07:11.460
and that gain to the system,
that positive valance

01:07:11.460 --> 01:07:14.430
that emerges out of that negative signal

01:07:14.430 --> 01:07:19.230
is sufficient to create
that positive association.

01:07:19.230 --> 01:07:21.843
And in the case of beer,
of course, it's alcohol.

01:07:22.740 --> 01:07:25.230
The feeling good that we get after

01:07:25.230 --> 01:07:28.923
is more than sufficient to say,
I want to have more of this.

01:07:30.348 --> 01:07:31.350
And in the case of coffee, of course,

01:07:31.350 --> 01:07:34.440
is caffeine activating a whole
group of neurotransmitter

01:07:34.440 --> 01:07:39.390
systems that give you that
high associated with coffee.

01:07:39.390 --> 01:07:42.841
So yes, this taste system is changeable.

01:07:42.841 --> 01:07:45.179
It's malleable, and it's subject subjected

01:07:45.179 --> 01:07:46.163
to learning and experience.

01:07:47.951 --> 01:07:50.230
But I like the olfactory
system is restricted

01:07:51.570 --> 01:07:53.220
in what you could do with it

01:07:53.220 --> 01:07:57.120
because its goal is to allow you

01:07:57.120 --> 01:07:59.580
to get nutrients and survive.

01:07:59.580 --> 01:08:02.640
The goal of the olfactory
system is very different.

01:08:02.640 --> 01:08:04.770
It's being used, not in our case,

01:08:04.770 --> 01:08:06.520
but in every animal species

01:08:07.603 --> 01:08:09.753
to identify friend versus foe,

01:08:11.598 --> 01:08:12.453
to identify mate,

01:08:15.258 --> 01:08:18.870
to identify ecological
niches they want to be in.

01:08:18.870 --> 01:08:23.870
So it plays a very broad
role that then requires

01:08:24.390 --> 01:08:26.820
that it be set up organized

01:08:26.820 --> 01:08:30.600
and function in a very
different type of context.

01:08:30.600 --> 01:08:35.160
Taste is about, can we get the
nutrients we need to survive?

01:08:35.160 --> 01:08:38.790
And can we ensure that we are
attracted to the ones we need?

01:08:38.790 --> 01:08:41.440
And we adverse to the ones
that are going to kill us,

01:08:42.420 --> 01:08:44.670
I'm being overly simplistic
and reductionist.

01:08:46.031 --> 01:08:47.340
But I think it illustrates
the huge difference

01:08:47.340 --> 01:08:50.640
between these two chemo sensory systems.

01:08:50.640 --> 01:08:53.010
- I don't think you're
being overly simplicity.

01:08:53.010 --> 01:08:54.657
I think it illustrates

01:08:54.657 --> 01:08:58.017
the key intractable nature of this system

01:08:58.017 --> 01:09:00.154
and the way you've approached it.

01:09:00.154 --> 01:09:01.890
And I think it's important
for people to hear that,

01:09:01.890 --> 01:09:02.910
because everybody,

01:09:02.910 --> 01:09:07.440
as we are as mystified with
empathy and love, et cetera.

01:09:07.440 --> 01:09:09.300
So in a fairness to that,

01:09:09.300 --> 01:09:10.720
I'm going to ask a sort of

01:09:11.824 --> 01:09:14.193
a high level question
or abstract question.

