01:10:15.150 --> 01:10:16.083
And she said,

01:10:16.943 --> 01:10:19.343
"I have a feeling something
about it is in smell

01:10:20.340 --> 01:10:23.370
and something about it
is actually in taste,

01:10:23.370 --> 01:10:25.590
literally the taste of somebody's breath."

01:10:25.590 --> 01:10:27.643
That's the way she described it.

01:10:27.643 --> 01:10:31.050
And I thought that it was
a very interesting example

01:10:31.050 --> 01:10:33.630
for a number of reasons,
but in particular,

01:10:33.630 --> 01:10:37.380
because it gets to the
merging of odor and taste.

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:40.950
But also to the idea that
of course the context

01:10:40.950 --> 01:10:43.860
of a new relationship, I'm
assuming that, and in fact,

01:10:43.860 --> 01:10:45.540
they're both attractive people, et cetera.

01:10:45.540 --> 01:10:46.710
There's a whole context there,

01:10:46.710 --> 01:10:51.100
but I've had the experience of the odor

01:10:52.260 --> 01:10:53.700
of somebody's breath being aversive.

01:10:53.700 --> 01:10:57.300
Not because I could
identify it as aversive.

01:10:57.300 --> 01:10:59.064
- Because you just didn't like it.

01:10:59.064 --> 01:11:01.200
- But because I just didn't like it.

01:11:01.200 --> 01:11:03.220
- But that's because you associated

01:11:04.590 --> 01:11:09.590
with added odors that trigger

01:11:10.650 --> 01:11:15.650
that negative aversive
reaction, by the way.

01:11:16.140 --> 01:11:16.973
- Absolutely.

01:11:17.807 --> 01:11:18.990
There are certain perfumes
to me that are aversive.

01:11:18.990 --> 01:11:20.893
- [Charles] You got it.

01:11:20.893 --> 01:11:22.483
- And there are other sense,

01:11:22.483 --> 01:11:25.680
I can recall a sense of skin, of foods,

01:11:25.680 --> 01:11:28.290
et cetera, that are immensely appetitive.

01:11:28.290 --> 01:11:31.470
So I've experienced both
sides of this equation myself.

01:11:31.470 --> 01:11:32.880
And she was describing this

01:11:32.880 --> 01:11:35.700
and to me more than tasting wine,

01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:37.200
which is the typical example

01:11:37.200 --> 01:11:39.720
where people inhale it and
then they drink it, to me,

01:11:39.720 --> 01:11:40.970
this seems like something

01:11:42.610 --> 01:11:43.703
that more people might
be able to relate to,

01:11:45.123 --> 01:11:47.520
that certain things and
people smell delicious.

01:11:47.520 --> 01:11:49.860
Even mothers describing the
smell of their baby's head.

01:11:49.860 --> 01:11:50.910
- A mother, or us.

01:11:50.910 --> 01:11:53.380
- [Andrew] Of course, men too, yeah.

01:11:53.380 --> 01:11:55.350
- I mean our own babies
when in their necks,

01:11:55.350 --> 01:11:56.850
that's the magical place.

01:11:56.850 --> 01:11:58.289
- Their neck.

01:11:58.289 --> 01:11:59.397
- The back of their neck.

01:11:59.397 --> 01:12:00.540
- There you go.
- Oh my goodness.

01:12:00.540 --> 01:12:01.560
I have a grandchild now.

01:12:01.560 --> 01:12:05.490
So I know exactly what, Rio,
that's his name, smells like.

01:12:05.490 --> 01:12:06.750
- Okay.

01:12:06.750 --> 01:12:07.583
So more beautiful examples.

01:12:07.583 --> 01:12:09.024
It's always more fun to think

01:12:09.024 --> 01:12:11.580
about the beautiful positively
appetitive examples.

01:12:11.580 --> 01:12:15.540
The smell of the back
of your grandson's neck.

01:12:15.540 --> 01:12:18.003
I mean, you could get
more specific than that,

01:12:18.957 --> 01:12:20.460
but not a lot more specific exactly.

01:12:20.460 --> 01:12:24.030
So what is going on in terms
of the combination of odor

01:12:24.030 --> 01:12:27.630
and taste given that these
two systems are so different?

01:12:27.630 --> 01:12:31.653
- Yes, and they come together,

01:12:32.610 --> 01:12:36.570
ultimately there is a place
in the brain where they come

01:12:36.570 --> 01:12:40.320
together to integrate the two into

01:12:40.320 --> 01:12:45.240
what we would call that
sensory experience.

01:12:45.240 --> 01:12:46.620
And I'll tell you an experiment

01:12:46.620 --> 01:12:50.239
that you could do that demonstrates this.

01:12:50.239 --> 01:12:52.494
I think it's good for the,

01:12:52.494 --> 01:12:55.320
for your audience here to get
a sense of how we approach

01:12:55.320 --> 01:12:57.210
these problems so that we can get

01:12:57.210 --> 01:12:59.163
in a meaningful scientific answers.

01:13:01.659 --> 01:13:05.130
So we know where the olfactory
cortex is in the brain.

01:13:05.130 --> 01:13:07.080
We know where the taste
cortex is in the brain.

01:13:07.080 --> 01:13:09.210
They're in two different places.

01:13:09.210 --> 01:13:12.663
We can go to each of these two, cortexes,

01:13:13.770 --> 01:13:17.520
put color traces, we put green in one,

01:13:17.520 --> 01:13:21.960
we put red in the other and
we see where the colors go to.

01:13:21.960 --> 01:13:23.360
That's a reflection of where

01:13:25.263 --> 01:13:29.160
those neurons are projecting
into their next targets.

01:13:29.160 --> 01:13:32.253
Once they get the signal, where
do they send the signal to?

01:13:33.270 --> 01:13:36.240
And then we reason that if odor

01:13:36.240 --> 01:13:38.970
and taste come together
somewhere in the brain,

01:13:38.970 --> 01:13:40.590
we should find an area that now

01:13:40.590 --> 01:13:42.753
it's getting red and green color.

01:13:44.190 --> 01:13:45.963
And we found such an area.

01:13:47.610 --> 01:13:49.890
And next we anticipated,

01:13:49.890 --> 01:13:53.070
we hypothesized that
maybe this is the area

01:13:53.070 --> 01:13:55.260
in the brain of the mouse,

01:13:55.260 --> 01:13:57.360
corresponding area in the brain of humans,

01:13:58.907 --> 01:14:03.090
that integrates odor and taste.

01:14:03.090 --> 01:14:04.500
It's known as,

01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:08.073
the term normally used is
multisensory integration.

01:14:08.970 --> 01:14:12.003
And if this is true, we could
do the following experiment.

01:14:12.960 --> 01:14:14.560
We can train a mouse

01:14:17.040 --> 01:14:20.643
to lick sweet.

01:14:22.845 --> 01:14:23.970
And if they guess correctly,

01:14:23.970 --> 01:14:25.860
that that is supposed to be sweet.

01:14:25.860 --> 01:14:30.860
They should go now to the
right port, to the right side,

01:14:31.260 --> 01:14:32.763
to get a water reward.

01:14:34.530 --> 01:14:38.160
If they go to the lift, when it was sweet,

01:14:38.160 --> 01:14:42.180
then they're incorrect
and they get no reward

01:14:42.180 --> 01:14:44.043
and they actually get a time out.

01:14:44.880 --> 01:14:46.620
Now the mice are thirsty,

01:14:46.620 --> 01:14:49.020
so they're very motivated to perform.

01:14:49.020 --> 01:14:52.440
And if you repeat this
task a hundred times,

01:14:52.440 --> 01:14:55.110
a hundred trials, incredibly enough,

01:14:55.110 --> 01:14:58.050
this animal learned to recognize the sweet

01:14:58.050 --> 01:15:00.120
and execute the right action.

01:15:00.120 --> 01:15:02.130
And by their action,

01:15:02.130 --> 01:15:07.083
we now are being told what
that animal is tasting.

01:15:08.010 --> 01:15:09.330
We can make it more interesting

01:15:09.330 --> 01:15:11.190
and we can give him
sweet and bitter and say,

01:15:11.190 --> 01:15:12.670
if it's sweet go to the right

01:15:13.764 --> 01:15:15.360
and if it's bitter go to the left.

01:15:15.360 --> 01:15:18.630
And after you train him
this mice with 90% accuracy,

01:15:18.630 --> 01:15:22.200
it'll tell you, when you
randomize now the stimuli,

01:15:22.200 --> 01:15:24.003
what was sweet and what was bitter?

01:15:25.560 --> 01:15:27.483
We can now do the same experiment,

01:15:28.320 --> 01:15:33.320
but now mix taste with odor and say,

01:15:33.660 --> 01:15:36.963
if you got odor alone,

01:15:38.370 --> 01:15:43.370
go to the right or push
this lever in mice.

01:15:43.440 --> 01:15:45.870
If you get taste alone,

01:15:45.870 --> 01:15:48.513
go to this other part or
push this other lever.

01:15:49.650 --> 01:15:53.580
And if you get the two together,
do this something else.

01:15:53.580 --> 01:15:55.020
And if you train the mice,

01:15:55.020 --> 01:15:58.140
the mice are able now to
report back to you when it's

01:15:58.140 --> 01:16:03.140
sensing taste alone,
odor alone, or the mix.

01:16:05.010 --> 01:16:07.220
Makes sense?
- Makes sense.

01:16:07.220 --> 01:16:09.760
- Now we can go to the
brain of this mice and go

01:16:10.650 --> 01:16:13.050
to this area that we now uncover,

01:16:13.050 --> 01:16:17.160
discover as being the side
of multisensory integration

01:16:17.160 --> 01:16:21.153
between taste and odor, and silence it,

01:16:22.020 --> 01:16:26.375
prevent it from being
activated experimentally.

01:16:26.375 --> 01:16:29.520
And if that area really represented

01:16:29.520 --> 01:16:31.620
the integration of these two,

01:16:31.620 --> 01:16:33.090
the animals should still be able

01:16:33.090 --> 01:16:35.160
to recognize the taste alone.

01:16:35.160 --> 01:16:38.190
They still should be able to
recognize the other alone,

01:16:38.190 --> 01:16:42.993
but should be incapable
now to recognize the mix,

01:16:44.550 --> 01:16:46.950
and exactly as predicted.

01:16:46.950 --> 01:16:49.335
That's exactly what you get.

01:16:49.335 --> 01:16:50.220
All right?

01:16:50.220 --> 01:16:51.720
- The brain is basically

01:16:51.720 --> 01:16:55.470
a series of engineered circuits, complex.

01:16:55.470 --> 01:16:56.812
- You got it.

01:16:56.812 --> 01:17:01.097
And our task is to figure
out how can we extract

01:17:03.180 --> 01:17:07.033
this amazing architecture
of these circuits

01:17:07.033 --> 01:17:10.020
in a way that we can begin to uncover

01:17:10.020 --> 01:17:12.960
the mysteries of the brain.

01:17:12.960 --> 01:17:16.506
- And why certain people's
breath tastes so good

01:17:16.506 --> 01:17:18.750
and other people's not so good.

01:17:18.750 --> 01:17:19.583
- Exactly.

01:17:19.583 --> 01:17:20.416
So I never answered that,

01:17:20.416 --> 01:17:22.080
but I told you how we can figure out

01:17:22.080 --> 01:17:24.693
where in the brain it's happening.

01:17:25.860 --> 01:17:28.185
- As we've been having this discussion,

01:17:28.185 --> 01:17:31.180
I thought a few times about similarities

01:17:32.106 --> 01:17:34.110
to the visual system or
differences to the visual system,

01:17:34.110 --> 01:17:34.943
the visual system,

01:17:34.943 --> 01:17:38.170
there are a couple of
phenomenon that I wonder

01:17:39.402 --> 01:17:41.693
if they also exist in the taste system,

01:17:41.693 --> 01:17:43.860
and the visual system
we know for instance,

01:17:43.860 --> 01:17:44.693
that if you look at something long enough

01:17:44.693 --> 01:17:47.040
and activate the given
receptors long enough,

01:17:47.040 --> 01:17:49.350
that object will actually disappear.

01:17:49.350 --> 01:17:51.360
We offset this with little
micro eye movements,

01:17:51.360 --> 01:17:53.730
et cetera, but the principle
is a fundamental one.

01:17:53.730 --> 01:17:55.770
This habituation or desensitization,

01:17:55.770 --> 01:17:57.630
everyone seems to call
it something different,

01:17:57.630 --> 01:18:00.870
but you get the idea of course,

01:18:00.870 --> 01:18:02.313
in the taste system,

01:18:03.210 --> 01:18:05.250
I'm certainly familiar
with eating something very,

01:18:05.250 --> 01:18:07.950
very sweet for the first
time in a long time.

01:18:07.950 --> 01:18:10.800
And it feel tastes very
sweet, but a few more licks,

01:18:10.800 --> 01:18:11.670
a few more bites,

01:18:11.670 --> 01:18:15.090
and now it tastes not as
sweet, with olfaction,

01:18:15.090 --> 01:18:17.520
I'm familiar with the odor
in a room I don't like,

01:18:17.520 --> 01:18:18.930
or I like, and then it disappearing.

01:18:18.930 --> 01:18:23.133
So similar phenomena,
where does that occur?

01:18:24.949 --> 01:18:29.010
And can you imagine a sort of a system

01:18:29.010 --> 01:18:31.620
by which people could leverage that?

01:18:31.620 --> 01:18:35.448
Because I do think that
most people are interested

01:18:35.448 --> 01:18:38.638
in eating, not more sugar, but less sugar.

01:18:38.638 --> 01:18:39.537
- I think we have better
ways to approach that.

01:18:39.537 --> 01:18:44.537
And we can transition from
taste into these other circuits

01:18:44.550 --> 01:18:49.550
that makes sugar so extraordinarily

01:18:50.040 --> 01:18:53.580
impossible not to consume.

01:18:53.580 --> 01:18:55.593
- Impossible, exactly.

01:18:57.658 --> 01:19:02.010
- So where does the desensitizing happen.

01:19:02.010 --> 01:19:07.010
That's the term that we use, and it's,

01:19:08.598 --> 01:19:11.970
I think happening at multiple stations,

01:19:11.970 --> 01:19:15.570
it's happening at the receptor level.

01:19:15.570 --> 01:19:18.420
I.e. the salt in your tongue

01:19:18.420 --> 01:19:20.133
that are sensing the sugar,

01:19:21.540 --> 01:19:23.340
as you activate this receptor

01:19:23.340 --> 01:19:27.060
and it's triggering activity
after activity after activity,

01:19:27.060 --> 01:19:30.120
eventually you exhaust the receptor again,

01:19:30.120 --> 01:19:33.723
I'm using terms which are
extraordinarily loose.

01:19:34.920 --> 01:19:36.867
- But for sake of this discussion.

01:19:36.867 --> 01:19:39.060
- For the sake of this
discussion the receptor

01:19:40.200 --> 01:19:44.010
gets to a point where he
undergoes a set of changes,

01:19:44.010 --> 01:19:49.010
chemical changes, where it now
signals far less efficiently,

01:19:50.640 --> 01:19:55.587
or it even gets removed from
the surface of the cell.

01:19:55.587 --> 01:19:58.350
And now what will happen
is that the same amount

01:19:58.350 --> 01:20:02.763
of sugar will trigger
far less of a response.

01:20:03.869 --> 01:20:08.073
And that is a huge side
of this modulation.

01:20:09.240 --> 01:20:12.930
And then the next, I believe
is the integrated again,

01:20:12.930 --> 01:20:17.310
loss of signaling that happens
by continuous activation

01:20:17.310 --> 01:20:20.910
of the circuit at each of these
different neural stations.

01:20:20.910 --> 01:20:23.760
You know, there is from
the tongue to the ganglia,

01:20:23.760 --> 01:20:26.078
from the ganglia to the first station

01:20:26.078 --> 01:20:29.130
in the brain stem, a second
station in the brain stem,

01:20:29.130 --> 01:20:32.250
to the thalamus, then to the cortex.

01:20:32.250 --> 01:20:35.190
So there are multiple steps
that this signal is traveling.

01:20:35.190 --> 01:20:37.890
Now, you might say why,
if this is a label line,

01:20:37.890 --> 01:20:41.070
why do you need to have so many stations?

01:20:41.070 --> 01:20:43.890
And that's because the taste
system is so important to

01:20:43.890 --> 01:20:48.030
ensure that you get what
you need to survive,

01:20:48.030 --> 01:20:49.530
that it has to be subjected

01:20:49.530 --> 01:20:53.220
to modulation by the internal state.

01:20:53.220 --> 01:20:57.330
And each of these nodes
provides a new site

01:20:57.330 --> 01:21:00.930
to give it plasticity and modulation,

01:21:00.930 --> 01:21:04.083
not necessarily to change the
way that something tastes,

01:21:05.040 --> 01:21:08.730
but to ensure that you consume more

01:21:08.730 --> 01:21:12.900
or less or differently of what you need.

01:21:12.900 --> 01:21:14.910
I'm going to give you one example

01:21:14.910 --> 01:21:16.440
of how the internal state changes

01:21:16.440 --> 01:21:18.123
the way the taste system works.

01:21:19.350 --> 01:21:24.350
Salt is very appetitive
at low concentrations.

01:21:25.380 --> 01:21:26.883
And that's because we need it.

01:21:28.140 --> 01:21:31.020
It's our electro lite
balance requires salt.

01:21:31.020 --> 01:21:33.090
Every one of our neurons uses salt

01:21:33.090 --> 01:21:35.610
as the most important of the ions

01:21:35.610 --> 01:21:38.460
with potassium to ensure
that you can transfer

01:21:38.460 --> 01:21:41.793
these electrical signals
within and between neurons,

01:21:44.400 --> 01:21:46.110
but at high concentrations,

01:21:46.110 --> 01:21:50.850
let's say ocean water,
it's incredibly aversive.

01:21:50.850 --> 01:21:53.705
And we all know this because
we are going to the ocean.

01:21:53.705 --> 01:21:56.003
And then when you get it in
your mouth, it's not that great.

01:21:57.390 --> 01:22:02.040
However, if I salt deprive
you, and we can do this

01:22:02.040 --> 01:22:05.223
in experimental models quite readily,

01:22:07.440 --> 01:22:10.383
now this incredibly high
concentration of salt,

01:22:11.269 --> 01:22:13.500
one molar sodium chloride becomes

01:22:13.500 --> 01:22:15.933
amazingly appetitive and attractive.

01:22:17.040 --> 01:22:19.170
What's going on in here.

01:22:19.170 --> 01:22:22.500
Your tongue is telling
you, this is horrible,

01:22:22.500 --> 01:22:26.400
but your brain is telling
you, I don't care.

01:22:26.400 --> 01:22:27.393
You need it.

01:22:29.730 --> 01:22:33.360
And this is what we call the modulation

01:22:33.360 --> 01:22:37.650
of the taste system by the internal state.

01:22:37.650 --> 01:22:39.420
- And presumably if one is hungry enough,

01:22:39.420 --> 01:22:41.073
even uni will taste good.

01:22:42.822 --> 01:22:43.904
Just hit you hit it right on the money.

01:22:43.904 --> 01:22:45.990
No, no, this is exactly correct.

01:22:45.990 --> 01:22:48.333
Or if you're thirsty and hungry,

01:22:49.740 --> 01:22:53.070
you suppress hunger so that you don't

01:22:53.070 --> 01:22:56.888
waste water molecules in digesting food.

01:22:56.888 --> 01:22:57.960
Why?

01:22:57.960 --> 01:23:01.380
Because if you're thirsty
and you have no water,

01:23:01.380 --> 01:23:03.303
you will die within a week or so,

01:23:04.380 --> 01:23:06.570
but you can on a hunger strike as long

01:23:06.570 --> 01:23:10.680
as you have water for months,

01:23:10.680 --> 01:23:14.580
because you're going to eat
up all your energy reserves.

01:23:14.580 --> 01:23:16.020
Water is a different story.

01:23:16.020 --> 01:23:16.950
So you could see,

01:23:16.950 --> 01:23:20.220
or that there are multiple layers

01:23:20.220 --> 01:23:25.050
at which the taste system that guides

01:23:25.050 --> 01:23:28.260
our drive and our motivation to consume

01:23:28.260 --> 01:23:33.030
the nutrients we need has to be modulated

01:23:33.030 --> 01:23:35.790
in response to the internal state.

01:23:35.790 --> 01:23:37.256
And of course,

01:23:37.256 --> 01:23:38.089
internal state itself has

01:23:38.089 --> 01:23:41.783
to be modulated by the external world.

01:23:41.783 --> 01:23:45.630
And so that I think is a reason
why, what could otherwise,

01:23:45.630 --> 01:23:47.580
would've been an incredibly simple system

01:23:47.580 --> 01:23:52.530
from the tongue to the
cortex in one just wire.

01:23:52.530 --> 01:23:55.210
It's not, because you have to ensure

01:23:56.084 --> 01:23:59.310
that each step you give the system

01:23:59.310 --> 01:24:01.470
that level of flexibility

01:24:01.470 --> 01:24:04.053
or what we call in
neuroscience, plasticity.

01:24:05.160 --> 01:24:07.260
- I think we're headed into the gut.

01:24:07.260 --> 01:24:10.290
But I have a question that has just been

01:24:10.290 --> 01:24:12.003
on my mind for a bit now,

01:24:12.870 --> 01:24:15.000
because I was drinking this water

01:24:15.000 --> 01:24:17.103
and it has essentially no taste.

01:24:18.330 --> 01:24:22.500
Is there any kind of signal for
the absence of taste despite

01:24:22.500 --> 01:24:25.233
having something in the
mouth and here is why I ask,

01:24:26.458 --> 01:24:27.893
what I'm thinking about is saliva.

01:24:29.729 --> 01:24:31.620
And while it's true that if
I eat a lot of very highly

01:24:31.620 --> 01:24:34.320
palatable foods that does change

01:24:34.320 --> 01:24:37.620
how I experience more bland foods,

01:24:37.620 --> 01:24:40.380
I must confess when I eat a
lot of these highly processed

01:24:40.380 --> 01:24:41.790
foods I don't particularly like them.

01:24:41.790 --> 01:24:43.170
I tend to crave healthier foods,

